hadiths[9]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1346","It is narrated from Usama bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A Muslim cannot inherit from a disbeliever, nor can a disbeliever inherit from a Muslim."
"1347","Ali bin Husayn (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated that the heirs of Abu Talib were Aqil and Talib; Ali and Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with them both) did not inherit. He said: That is why we gave up our share of Sha'b Abi Talib."
"1348","It is narrated from Amr bin Shu'aib that a man from Banu Mudlij, Qatadah, struck his son or struck him with a sword (the narrator is unsure), which hit his shin, and blood kept flowing from the wound until he died. Then Suraqah bin Ju'sham (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned this incident to him. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When I come to you, keep 120 camels ready at the place of Qudayd. Then when Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived, he took 30 haqqahs, 30 jadh'ahs, and 40 pregnant she-camels from those camels and said, ""Where is the brother of the slain?"" He said, ""I am here."" Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Take them, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The killer has no share (in inheritance or blood money).'”"
"1349","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that 'Umar bin al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say that blood money is obligatory upon the heirs, and a wife will not inherit anything from her husband's blood money. Until Dahhak bin Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had written to him instructing that the wife of Ashyam al-Dhibabi (may Allah be pleased with him) should be made an heir to his blood money. Then 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) retracted his previous position."
"1350","It is narrated from Ibn Shihab that the Prophet ﷺ wrote to Dahhak bin Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) that make the wife of Ashyam al-Dhababi an heir from his blood money."
"1351","Ibn Abi Mulaykah reports that he asked Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them) about a man who gives his wife an irrevocable (bain) divorce and then dies while the woman is still in her waiting period (‘iddah). Abdullah ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them) replied: Abdur Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) gave Tamadir bint Asbagh Kalbiyyah an irrevocable divorce, then he died while she was still in her waiting period, so Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) made her an heir. Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them) said: As for me, I think that a woman who has been given an irrevocable divorce should not be made an heir."
"1352","Talha bin Abdur Rahman bin Awf narrated, and he was the most knowledgeable about this issue, and from Abu Salamah bin Abdur Rahman bin Awf, it is narrated that Abdur Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) gave his wife a final (irrevocable) divorce during his illness. So Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) made her an heir to his wealth after her waiting period had ended."
"1353","It is narrated from Mujahid (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no bequest for an heir."
"1354","It is narrated from Ibn Jurayj (may Allah have mercy on him) that Tawus and Ata (may Allah have mercy on them both) stated: ""The obligatory Hajj (which was due upon the deceased) will be fulfilled from the principal wealth."
